@subsection Expressions

One easy way to get started at programming is to examine some typical
expressions of a program and the results they produce.  Imagine that you are
sitting at a computer terminal.  You present an @newterm{expression} to a
running program, and it responds by displaying the result of
@newterm{evaluating} that expression.  Throughout this book, an expression
appears in Kotlin, and the value it evaluates to appears just below it as a
comment line carrying a @code{=>} marker, in the convention of @ref{0.8}.

One kind of primitive expression you might present is a number.  (More
precisely, the expression that you write consists of the numerals that
represent the number in base 10.)  If you present the program with a number

@example kotlin
486L
// => 486
@end example

@noindent
the value printed is the number itself.@footnote{Throughout this book, when
we wish to emphasize the distinction between the expression written by the
author and the value the program produces, we show the latter as a comment
line introduced by @code{=>}.}

Expressions representing numbers may be combined with an expression
representing a primitive operation (such as @code{+} or @code{*}) to form a
compound expression that represents the application of the operation to those
numbers.  For @w{example}:

@example kotlin
137L + 349L
// => 486

1000L - 334L
// => 666

5L * 99L
// => 495

10L / 5L
// => 2

2.7 + 10.0
// => 12.7
@end example

@noindent
Expressions such as these, formed by placing an operator between operand
expressions so as to denote the application of an operation, are called
@newterm{combinations}.  The book's original language wrote its operators in
front of their operands: it called the leftmost element the
@newterm{operator}, the other elements the @newterm{operands}, and the
convention @newterm{prefix notation}.  Kotlin spells its arithmetic operators
between the operands, in the customary mathematical way, and reserves the
prefix position for names: a function such as @code{square} is written before
the parentheses that hold its arguments, so every operator of this chapter is
also a function, viewed through its infix spelling.  The value of a
combination is obtained by applying the operation specified by the operator
to the @newterm{arguments} that are the values of the operands.

Two properties of the book's prefix notation are worth carrying across,
because Kotlin's notation preserves both.  The first is that an operation may
take any number of arguments; a sum of four numbers is written as a chain of
the same operator:

@example kotlin
21L + 35L + 12L + 7L
// => 75

25L * 4L * 12L
// => 1200
@end example

@noindent
The second is that no ambiguity can arise: the language fixes the precedence
and associativity of every operator, so the sum above groups as
@code{((21L + 35L) + 12L) + 7L}, and parentheses may always make the intended
grouping explicit.

A second advantage of prefix notation extends in a straightforward way to
allow combinations to be @i{nested}, that is, to have combinations whose
elements are themselves combinations.  Kotlin nests the same way:

@example kotlin
3L * 5L + (10L - 6L)
// => 19
@end example

@noindent
There is no limit (in principle) to the depth of such nesting and to the
overall complexity of the expressions that the Kotlin compiler can evaluate.
It is we humans who get confused by still relatively simple expressions such
as

@example kotlin
3L * (2L * 4L + (3L + 5L)) + (10L - 7L + 6L)
@end example

@noindent
which the program would readily evaluate to be 57.  We can help ourselves by
writing such an expression in the form

@example kotlin
3L * (
    2L * 4L +
        (3L + 5L)
) +
    ((10L - 7L) + 6L)
// => 57
@end example

@noindent
following a formatting convention known as @newterm{pretty-printing}, in
which each long combination is written so that the operands are aligned
vertically.  The resulting indentations display clearly the structure of the
expression.@footnote{Kotlin development environments typically provide
features to aid the user in formatting expressions.  Two especially useful
features are one that automatically indents to the proper pretty-print
position whenever a new line is started and one that highlights the matching
left bracket whenever a right bracket is typed.  This book's own listings are
kept in a form its formatter accepts, so the shape shown is the shape the
machine checks.}

Even with complex expressions, evaluation always operates in the same basic
cycle: the compiler resolves each name against the environment of the
program, the running program evaluates the expression, and the value is
recorded.  An interactive Lisp system runs this cycle continuously and calls
it a @newterm{read-eval-print loop}; the listings of this book play the
loop's role one interaction at a time, and nothing in a listing is
hand-copied: the file under @file{kotlin/examples/} that the listing names
computes and asserts every value shown.@footnote{Kotlin comes close to
obeying the convention that every expression has a value; a statement such as
a loop carries the uninformative one, the type @code{Unit}.  The convention,
together with the old reputation of Lisp as an inefficient language, is the
source of the quip by Alan Perlis (paraphrasing Oscar Wilde) that ``Lisp
programmers know the value of everything but the cost of nothing.''}

@node	1.1.2, 1.1.3, 1.1.1, 1.1
@subsection Naming and the Environment

A critical aspect of a programming language is the means it provides for using
names to refer to computational objects.  We say that the name identifies a
@newterm{variable} whose @newterm{value} is the object.

In Kotlin, we name things with @code{val}, which binds a name that no later
binding in the same scope can rebind (@ref{0.2}).  Writing

@example kotlin
val size = 2L
@end example

@noindent
binds the name @code{size} to the value 2.@footnote{A definition produces no
value to record, which is why the book annotates only expressions.}  Once the
name @code{size} has been bound to the number 2, we can refer to the value 2
by name:

@example kotlin
size
// => 2

5L * size
// => 10
@end example

@noindent
Here are further examples of the use of @code{val}:

@example kotlin
val pi = 3.14159
val radius = 10L

pi * (radius * radius)
// => 314.159

val circumference = 2L * pi * radius

circumference
// => 62.8318
@end example

@noindent
@code{Val} is our language's simplest means of abstraction, for it allows us
to use simple names to refer to the results of compound operations, such as
the @code{circumference} computed above.  In general, computational objects
may have very complex structures, and it would be extremely inconvenient to
have to remember and repeat their details each time we want to use them.
Indeed, complex programs are constructed by building, step by step,
computational objects of increasing complexity.  Kotlin makes this
step-by-step program construction particularly convenient: name-object
associations are created incrementally across the small definitions of a
program, and the tests that accompany every listing re-run them on each
build.  This encourages the incremental development and testing of programs
and is largely responsible for the fact that a well-written Kotlin program
usually consists of a large number of relatively simple functions.

It should be clear that the possibility of associating values with names and
later retrieving them means that something must keep track of the
name-object pairs.  In the book's original language that something is a table
the interpreter consults while the program runs.  In Kotlin it is the
compiler's own record of the program's declarations, consulted when a name is
resolved; its shape is exactly the structure the book is about to name, and
@ref{Chapter 4} will implement it as a literal table.  This memory is called
the @newterm{environment} (more precisely the @newterm{global environment},
since we will see later that a computation may involve a number of different
environments).@footnote{@ref{Chapter 3} will show that this notion of
environment is crucial, both for understanding how the interpreter works and
for implementing interpreters.}

@node	1.1.3, 1.1.4, 1.1.2, 1.1
@subsection Evaluating Combinations

One of our goals in this chapter is to isolate issues about thinking
procedurally.  As a case in point, let us consider that, in evaluating
combinations, the evaluator is itself following a procedure.

@quotation
To evaluate a combination, do the following:

@enumerate 1

@item
Evaluate the subexpressions of the combination.

@item
Apply the procedure that is the value of the operator subexpression to the
arguments that are the values of the other subexpressions (the operands).

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@noindent
Even this simple rule illustrates some important points about processes in
general.  First, observe that the first step dictates that in order to
accomplish the evaluation process for a combination we must first perform the
evaluation process on each element of the combination.  Thus, the evaluation
rule is @newterm{recursive} in nature; that is, it includes, as one of its
steps, the need to invoke the rule itself.@footnote{It may seem strange that
the evaluation rule says, as part of the first step, that we should evaluate
the operator, since at this point an operator can only be a fixed infix
symbol such as @code{+} or @code{*} that names a primitive operation of the
type of its operands.  We will see later that it is useful to be able to work
with combinations whose operator position holds an arbitrary expression.}

Notice how succinctly the idea of recursion can be used to express what, in
the case of a deeply nested combination, would otherwise be viewed as a
rather complicated process.  For example, evaluating

@example kotlin
(2L + 4L * 6L) * (3L + 5L + 7L)
@end example

@noindent
requires that the evaluation rule be applied to four different combinations.
We can obtain a picture of this process by representing the combination in
the form of a tree, as shown in @ref{Figure 1.1}.  Each combination is
represented by a node with branches corresponding to the operator and the
operands of the combination stemming from it.  The terminal nodes (that is,
nodes with no branches stemming from them) represent either operators or
numbers.  Viewing evaluation in terms of the tree, we can imagine that the
values of the operands percolate upward, starting from the terminal nodes
and then combining at higher and higher levels.  In general, we shall see
that recursion is a very powerful technique for dealing with hierarchical,
treelike objects.  In fact, the ``percolate values upward'' form of the
evaluation rule is an example of a general kind of process known as
@newterm{tree accumulation}.

