@noindent
Next, observe that the repeated application of the first step brings us to
the point where we need to evaluate, not combinations, but primitive
expressions such as numerals, built-in operators, or other names.  We take
care of the primitive cases by stipulating that

@itemize @bullet

@item
the values of numerals are the numbers that they name,

@item
the values of built-in operators are the primitive operations of the number
types that carry out the corresponding operations, and

@item
the values of other names are the objects bound to those names in the
environment.

@end itemize

@noindent
In the book's original language the operator names live in the global
environment beside all the other names, and the second rule is a special case
of the third.  In Kotlin the arithmetic operators are fixed spellings for the
primitive operations of each type, which is the same fact seen from the
compiler's side.  The key point to notice is the role of the environment in
determining the meaning of the names in expressions.  In a compiled language
such as Kotlin, an expression such as @code{x + 1L} does not compile unless
the environment of the program provides a meaning for the name @code{x}; the
compiler rejects an unbound name before the program ever runs, rather than
stumbling over it at run time.  As we shall see in @ref{Chapter 3}, the
general notion of the environment as providing a context in which evaluation
takes place will play an important role in our understanding of program
execution.

Notice that the evaluation rule given above does not handle definitions.
For instance, evaluating @code{val x = 3L} does not bind @code{x} by
applying some procedure to two arguments, one of which is the value of the
symbol @code{x} and the other of which is 3, since the purpose of the
declaration is precisely to associate @code{x} with a value.  (That is,
@code{val x = 3L} is not an expression.)

Such exceptions to the general evaluation rule are called @newterm{special forms}.  A declaration is one example; we will meet others shortly, and
Kotlin has its own stock of them wherever the general rule would be wrong or
wasteful: the connective @code{&&} does not evaluate its right operand unless
it must, and a @code{when} evaluates only the arm it takes.  Each special
form has its own evaluation rule.  The various kinds of expressions (each
with its associated evaluation rule) constitute the syntax of the programming
language.  The full syntax of Kotlin is much larger than the syntax of Lisp,
which is famously small: one general evaluation rule plus specialized rules
for a handful of special forms.  But the expression language of this
chapter---arithmetic, names, calls, and conditionals---is described by
exactly one general rule and a small number of special
forms.@footnote{Special syntactic forms that are simply convenient
alternative surface structures for things that can be written in more uniform
ways are sometimes called @newterm{syntactic sugar}, to use a phrase coined
by Peter Landin.  In comparison with users of other languages, Lisp
programmers, as a rule, are less concerned with matters of syntax.  (By
contrast, examine any Pascal manual and notice how much of it is devoted to
descriptions of syntax.)  This disdain for syntax is due partly to the
flexibility of Lisp, which makes it easy to change surface syntax, and partly
to the observation that many ``convenient'' syntactic constructs, which make
the language less uniform, end up causing more trouble than they are worth
when programs become large and complex.  In the words of Alan Perlis,
``Syntactic sugar causes cancer of the semicolon.''}

@node	1.1.4, 1.1.5, 1.1.3, 1.1
@subsection Compound Procedures

We have identified in Kotlin some of the elements that must appear in any
powerful programming language:

@itemize @bullet

@item
Numbers and arithmetic operations are primitive data and procedures.

@item
Nesting of combinations provides a means of combining operations.

@item
Definitions that associate names with values provide a limited means of
abstraction.

@end itemize

@noindent
Now we will learn about @newterm{procedure definitions}, a much more powerful
abstraction technique by which a compound operation can be given a name and
then referred to as a unit.

We begin by examining how to express the idea of ``squaring.''  We might say,
``To square something, multiply it by itself.''  This is expressed in our
language as

@example kotlin
public fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x
@end example

@noindent
We can understand this in the following way:

@example
fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x
 |     |     |                 |
 To square something, multiply it by itself.
@end example

@noindent
We have here a @newterm{compound procedure}, which has been given the name
@code{square}.  The procedure represents the operation of multiplying
something by itself.  The thing to be multiplied is given a local name,
@code{x}, which plays the same role that a pronoun plays in natural language.
Evaluating the definition creates this compound procedure and associates it
with the name @code{square}.@footnote{Observe that there are two different
operations being combined here: we are creating the procedure, and we are
giving it the name @code{square}.  It is possible, indeed important, to be
able to separate these two notions---to create procedures without naming
them, and to give names to procedures that have already been created.  In
Kotlin an unnamed procedure is a lambda such as @code{@{ x -> x * x @}}, and
an existing named one can be handed around as a function reference; we will
see how to do this in @ref{1.3.2}.}

The general form of a procedure definition is

@example kotlin
public fun ⟨@var{name}⟩(⟨@var{formal parameters}⟩): ⟨@var{type}⟩ = ⟨@var{body}⟩
@end example

@noindent
The @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} is a symbol to be associated with the procedure definition in
the environment.@footnote{Throughout this book, we will describe the general
syntax of expressions by using italic symbols delimited by angle
brackets---e.g., @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩}---to denote the ``slots'' in the expression to be
filled in when such an expression is actually used.} The
@code{⟨}@var{formal parameters}@code{⟩} are the names used within the body of the function to
refer to the corresponding arguments of the function.  The
@code{⟨}@var{body}@code{⟩} is an expression that will yield the value of the procedure
application when the formal parameters are replaced by the actual arguments
to which the procedure is applied.@footnote{More generally, the body of a
function can be a braced block of statements; in that case the function's
value is stated with a @code{return}.} The formal parameters are grouped
within the parentheses that follow the name, just as they would appear in an
actual call to the function being defined.

Having defined @code{square}, we can now use it:

@example kotlin
square(21L)
// => 441

square(2L + 5L)
// => 49

square(square(3L))
// => 81
@end example

@noindent
We can also use @code{square} as a building block in defining other
procedures.  For example, @math{x^2 + y^2} can be expressed as

@example kotlin
square(x) + square(y)
@end example

@noindent
We can easily define a procedure @code{sumOfSquares} that, given any two
numbers as arguments, produces the sum of their squares:

@example kotlin
public fun sumOfSquares(x: Long, y: Long): Long = square(x) + square(y)

sumOfSquares(3L, 4L)
// => 25
@end example

@noindent
Now we can use @code{sumOfSquares} as a building block in constructing
further procedures:

@example kotlin
public fun f(a: Long): Long = sumOfSquares(a + 1L, a * 2L)

f(5L)
// => 136
@end example

@noindent
Compound procedures are used in exactly the same way as primitive procedures.
Indeed, one could not tell by looking at the definition of
@code{sumOfSquares} given above whether @code{square} came from the standard
library, like @code{+} and @code{*}, or was defined as a compound procedure.

@node	1.1.5, 1.1.6, 1.1.4, 1.1
@subsection The Substitution Model for Procedure Application

To evaluate a combination whose operator names a compound procedure, the
evaluator follows much the same process as for combinations whose operators
name primitive procedures, which we described in @ref{1.1.3}.  That is,
the evaluator evaluates the elements of the combination and applies the
procedure (which is the value of the operator of the combination) to the
arguments (which are the values of the operands of the combination).

We can assume that the mechanism for applying primitive procedures to
arguments is built into the evaluator.  For compound procedures, the
application process is as follows:

@quotation
To apply a compound procedure to arguments, evaluate the body of the
procedure with each formal parameter replaced by the corresponding argument.
@end quotation

@noindent
To illustrate this process, let's evaluate the combination

@example kotlin
f(5L)
@end example

@noindent
where @code{f} is the procedure defined in @ref{1.1.4}.  We begin by
retrieving the body of @code{f}:

@example kotlin
sumOfSquares(a + 1L, a * 2L)
@end example

@noindent
Then we replace the formal parameter @code{a} by the argument 5:

@example kotlin
sumOfSquares(5L + 1L, 5L * 2L)
@end example

@noindent
Thus the problem reduces to the evaluation of a combination with two operands
and an operator @code{sumOfSquares}.  Evaluating this combination involves
three subproblems.  We must evaluate the operator to get the procedure to be
applied, and we must evaluate the operands to get the arguments.  Now
@code{5L + 1L} produces 6 and @code{5L * 2L} produces 10, so we must apply
the @code{sumOfSquares} procedure to 6 and 10.  These values are substituted
for the formal parameters @code{x} and @code{y} in the body of
@code{sumOfSquares}, reducing the expression to

@example kotlin
square(6L) + square(10L)
@end example

@noindent
If we use the definition of @code{square}, this reduces to

@example kotlin
6L * 6L + 10L * 10L
@end example

@noindent
which reduces by multiplication to

@example kotlin
36L + 100L
@end example

@noindent
and finally to

@example kotlin
136
@end example

@noindent
The process we have just described is called the @newterm{substitution model}
for procedure application.  It can be taken as a model that determines the
``meaning'' of procedure application, insofar as the procedures in this
chapter are concerned.  However, there are two points that should be stressed:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The purpose of the substitution is to help us think about procedure
application, not to provide a description of how the evaluator really works.
The Kotlin compiler does not evaluate calls by manipulating the text of a
function to substitute values for the formal parameters.  In practice, the
``substitution'' is accomplished by giving each call its own frame of
parameter bindings.  We will discuss this more fully in @ref{Chapter 3} and
@ref{Chapter 4} when we examine the implementation of an interpreter in
detail.

@item
Over the course of this book, we will present a sequence of increasingly
elaborate models of how evaluators work, culminating with a complete
implementation of an interpreter and compiler in @ref{Chapter 5}.  The
substitution model is only the first of these models---a way to get started
thinking formally about the evaluation process.  In general, when modeling
phenomena in science and engineering, we begin with simplified, incomplete
models.  As we examine things in greater detail, these simple models become
inadequate and must be replaced by more refined models.  The substitution
model is no exception.  In particular, when we address in @ref{Chapter 3} the
use of procedures with ``mutable data,'' we will see that the substitution
model breaks down and must be replaced by a more complicated model of
procedure application.@footnote{Despite the simplicity of the substitution
idea, it turns out to be surprisingly complicated to give a rigorous
mathematical definition of the substitution process.  The problem arises from
the possibility of confusion between the names used for the formal parameters
of a procedure and the (possibly identical) names used in the expressions to
which the procedure may be applied.  Indeed, there is a long history of
erroneous definitions of @newterm{substitution} in the literature of logic
and programming semantics.  See @ref{Stoy 1977} for a careful discussion of
substitution.}

@end itemize

@subsubheading Applicative order versus normal order

According to the description of evaluation given in @ref{1.1.3}, the
evaluator first evaluates the operator and operands and then applies the
resulting procedure to the resulting arguments.  Kotlin commits to this
order---it is not one alternative among several but the only order a call
site has: every parameter of a function is an eager one, and its argument
expression is evaluated before the body runs.  This is called
@newterm{applicative-order evaluation}.  It is not the only way to perform
evaluation.  An alternative evaluation model would not evaluate the operands
until their values were needed.  Instead it would first substitute operand
expressions for parameters until it obtained an expression involving only
primitive operators, and would then perform the evaluation.  If we used this
method, the evaluation of @code{f(5L)} would proceed according to the
sequence of expansions

@example kotlin
sumOfSquares(5L + 1L, 5L * 2L)

square(5L + 1L) + square(5L * 2L)

(5L + 1L) * (5L + 1L) + (5L * 2L) * (5L * 2L)
@end example

@noindent
followed by the reductions

@example kotlin
6L * 6L + 10L * 10L

36L + 100L

136
@end example

@noindent
This gives the same answer as our previous evaluation model, but the process
is different.  In particular, the evaluations of @code{5L + 1L} and
@code{5L * 2L} are each performed twice here, corresponding to the reduction
of the expression @code{x * x} with @code{x} replaced respectively by
@code{5L + 1L} and @code{5L * 2L}.

This alternative ``fully expand and then reduce'' evaluation method is known
as @newterm{normal-order evaluation}, in contrast to the ``evaluate the
arguments and then apply'' method that Kotlin actually uses, applicative
order.  It can be shown that, for procedure applications that can be modeled
using substitution (including all the procedures in the first two chapters of
this book) and that yield legitimate values, normal-order and
applicative-order evaluation produce the same value.  (See @ref{Exercise 1.5}
for an instance of an ``illegitimate'' value where normal-order and
applicative-order evaluation do not give the same result.)

Normal order has no automatic form in Kotlin, but its central device---do not
evaluate an operand until its value is needed---is available explicitly, as
a parameter of function type.  The two shapes sit side by side:

@example kotlin
public fun testEager(x: Long, y: Long): Long = if (x == 0L) 0L else y

public fun testDeferred(x: Long, y: () -> Long): Long = if (x == 0L) 0L else y()
@end example

@noindent
An operand in the eager slot of @code{testEager} is evaluated even when
@code{x} is 0 and the body never reads @code{y}; an operand handed to
@code{testDeferred} is evaluated only if the body invokes it.  Which shape
matches the book's applicative-order interpreter, and which mimics normal
order for one argument, is the subject of @ref{Exercise 1.5}.  Applicative
order is the language's choice partly because of the additional efficiency
obtained from avoiding multiple evaluations of expressions such as those
illustrated with @code{5L + 1L} and @code{5L * 2L} above and, more
significantly, because normal-order evaluation becomes much more complicated
to deal with when we leave the realm of procedures that can be modeled by
substitution.  On the other hand, normal-order evaluation can be an extremely
valuable tool, and we will investigate some of its implications in
@ref{Chapter 3} and @ref{Chapter 4}.@footnote{In @ref{Chapter 3} we will
introduce @newterm{stream processing}, which is a way of handling apparently
``infinite'' data structures by incorporating a limited form of normal-order
evaluation.  In @ref{4.2} we will extend the evaluator of @ref{4.1} with
explicit thunks, producing a lazy variant of the book's language whose
arguments are deferred exactly where normal order defers them.}

@node	1.1.6, 1.1.7, 1.1.5, 1.1
@subsection Conditional Expressions and Predicates

The expressive power of the class of procedures that we can define at this
point is very limited, because we have no way to make tests and to perform
different operations depending on the result of a test.  For instance, we
cannot define a procedure that computes the absolute value of a number by
testing whether the number is positive, negative, or zero and taking different
actions in the different cases according to the rule
@displaymath
\left|{x}\right| \; = \;
  \cases{	 	 
       x & \hbox{if } $x > 0$, \cr
       0 & \hbox{if } $x  =  0$, \cr
      -x & \hbox{if } $x < 0$. 
    }
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
      /
      |   x  if x > 0
|x| = <   0  if x = 0
      |  -x  if x < 0
      \
@end example

@end ifinfo
This construct is called a @newterm{case analysis}, and Kotlin notates it
with a @code{when} expression:

@example kotlin
public fun abs(x: Long): Long =
    when @{
        x > 0L -> x
        x == 0L -> 0L
        else -> -x
    @}
@end example

@noindent
A @code{when} without a subject tries its conditions in order.  Kotlin
requires a @code{when} used as an expression to be exhaustive, so the
negation arm of the case analysis carries @code{else}; the explicit test
@code{x < 0L} would be redundant after the first two arms, and the compiler
accepts only the exhaustive spelling.

The general form of a conditional expression is

@example kotlin
when @{
    ⟨@var{p₁}⟩ -> ⟨@var{e₁}⟩
    ⟨@var{p₂}⟩ -> ⟨@var{e₂}⟩
    @r{…}
    else -> ⟨@var{eₙ}⟩
@}
@end example

@noindent
consisting of the symbol @code{when} followed by a sequence of arms

@example kotlin
⟨@var{p}⟩ -> ⟨@var{e}⟩
@end example

@noindent
called @newterm{clauses}. The expression before the arrow in each arm is a
@newterm{predicate}---that is, an expression whose value is interpreted as
either true or false.@footnote{``Interpreted as either true or false'' means
this: In Kotlin a condition must be a @code{Boolean}, and the only
@code{Boolean} values are the constants @code{true} and @code{false}; there
is no third value, and no other value is admitted where a condition is
expected.  (Scheme, the language of the original book, interprets any value
other than @code{#f} as true, which made providing @code{#t} logically
unnecessary but convenient.)}

Conditional expressions are evaluated as follows.  The predicate
@math{{⟨p_1⟩}} is evaluated first.  If its value is false, then
@math{{⟨p_2⟩}} is evaluated.  If @math{{⟨p_2⟩}}'s value is also false, then
@math{{⟨p_3⟩}} is evaluated.  This process continues until a predicate is
found whose value is true, in which case the evaluator returns the value of
the corresponding @newterm{consequent expression} @math{{⟨e⟩}} of the arm as
the value of the conditional expression.  If none of the @math{{⟨p⟩}}'s is
found to be true, the value of the @code{when} is the value of its
@code{else} arm; a @code{when} without @code{else} that falls through
produces the uninformative value @code{Unit}, and the compiler requires the
@code{else} whenever the result is to be used as a value.

The word @newterm{predicate} is used for procedures that return true or
false, as well as for expressions that evaluate to true or false.  The
absolute-value procedure @code{abs} makes use of the primitive predicates
@code{>}, @code{<}, and @code{==}.@footnote{@code{Abs} also uses the
``minus'' operator @code{-}, which, when written before its single operand,
as in @code{-x}, indicates negation.} These take two numbers as arguments and
test whether the first number is, respectively, greater than, less than, or
equal to the second number, returning true or false accordingly.

Another way to write the absolute-value procedure is

@example kotlin
public fun abs(x: Long): Long =
    when @{
        x < 0L -> -x
        else -> x
    @}
@end example

@noindent
which could be expressed in English as ``If @math{x} is less than zero return
@math{{-x}}; otherwise return @math{x}.''  @code{Else} is a keyword that can
be used in place of the final predicate of a @code{when}.  This causes the
@code{when} to return the value of the corresponding @math{{⟨e⟩}} whenever
all previous arms have been bypassed.

Here is yet another way to write the absolute-value procedure:

@example kotlin
public fun abs(x: Long): Long = if (x < 0L) -x else x
@end example

@noindent
This uses the special form @code{if}, a restricted type of conditional that
can be used when there are precisely two cases in the case analysis.  The
general form of an @code{if} expression is

@example kotlin
if (⟨@var{predicate}⟩) ⟨@var{consequent}⟩ else ⟨@var{alternative}⟩
@end example

@noindent
To evaluate an @code{if} expression, the evaluator starts by evaluating the
@code{⟨}@var{predicate}@code{⟩} part of the expression.  If the
@code{⟨}@var{predicate}@code{⟩} evaluates to a true value, the evaluator then
evaluates the @code{⟨}@var{consequent}@code{⟩} and returns its value.
Otherwise it evaluates the @code{⟨}@var{alternative}@code{⟩} and returns its
value.@footnote{A minor difference between @code{when} and @code{if} is that
a @code{when} arm may carry a guard, written @code{@var{p} where
@var{guard}}, which is tested after @var{p} succeeds; and the branches of
either form may be braced blocks, whose value is the value of their final
expression.}  Only the taken branch is evaluated, which is what makes
@code{if} a special form rather than an ordinary function.

In addition to primitive predicates such as @code{<}, @code{==}, and
@code{>}, there are logical composition operations, which enable us to
construct compound predicates.  The three most frequently used are these:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{⟨@var{e₁}⟩ && @r{…} && ⟨@var{eₙ}⟩}

The evaluator evaluates the operands one at a time, in left-to-right order.
If any operand evaluates to false, the value of the whole is false, and the
rest of the operands are not evaluated.  If all operands evaluate to true,
the value of the whole is the value of the last one.

@item
@code{⟨@var{e₁}⟩ || @r{…} || ⟨@var{eₙ}⟩}

The evaluator evaluates the operands one at a time, in left-to-right order.
If any operand evaluates to a true value, that value is returned as the value
of the whole, and the rest of the operands are not evaluated.  If all
operands evaluate to false, the value of the whole is false.

@item
@code{!(⟨@var{e}⟩)}

The value of a @code{!} expression is true when the expression
@code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} evaluates to false, and false otherwise.

@end itemize

@noindent
Notice that @code{&&} and @code{||} are special forms, not procedures,
because the operands are not necessarily all evaluated.  @code{!} is an
ordinary operator.

As an example of how these are used, the condition that a number @math{x} be
in the range @math{5 < x < 10} may be expressed as

@example kotlin
x > 5L && x < 10L
@end example

@noindent
As another example, we can define a predicate to test whether one number is
greater than or equal to another as

@example kotlin
public fun greaterOrEqual(x: Long, y: Long): Boolean = x > y || x == y
@end example

@noindent
or alternatively as

@example kotlin
public fun greaterOrEqual(x: Long, y: Long): Boolean = !(x < y)
@end example

@noindent
(The book redefines @code{>=} here; in one Kotlin file the second definition
would need a name of its own, and the listing's file gives it
@code{greaterOrEqualNot}.)

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.1}Exercise 1.1:} Below is a sequence of expressions.
What is the result of evaluating each expression?  Assume that the sequence is
to be evaluated in the order in which it is presented.

@example kotlin
10L

5L + 3L + 4L

